# Phase 6: Thread History, SSE Streaming, and Bookings Listing

## Background

The app currently has a single `POST /request` endpoint that runs the LangGraph workflow synchronously and returns the result as JSON. There is no persistent record of which threads belong to which user, no way to retrieve conversation message history, and no way to list a user's past bookings.

This plan adds four new capabilities:
1. **Thread listing** — `GET /threads/{user_id}` returns all threads with IDs and titles.
2. **Thread message history** — `GET /threads/{thread_id}/messages` returns a formatted conversation.
3. **SSE streaming for `/request`** — streams agent events in real-time instead of blocking.
4. **User bookings listing** — `GET /bookings/{user_id}` returns past bookings with provider name and status.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Thread Title Generation:** The plan generates a thread title by asking Gemini to summarize the first user message into a short (≤ 5 words) service request title (e.g., "AC Repair in G-13"). This adds one small Gemini call per new thread. If you prefer a simpler rule-based title (e.g., using the first 60 characters of the first message), we can do that instead with zero extra latency.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **SSE vs HTTP polling:** Moving `/request` to SSE means the client must consume a stream instead of a single JSON response. The response will be a series of `data: {...}` events ending with a `data: {"type": "done", ...}` event that carries the final state and `task_id`. Confirm this is acceptable for the mobile client (Expo's `fetch` can handle SSE via `EventSource` or a streaming fetch).

> [!NOTE]
> **`checkpoints.sqlite` vs `service_agent.db`:** Thread metadata (`threads` table) will be stored in `service_agent.db` alongside `providers` and `bookings` to keep all app data in one file. LangGraph conversation state stays in `checkpoints.sqlite`.

